<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="biostats-notes-5-2-independence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title> Tests for Independence </title>
    
    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Test for independence between random variables. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <definition xml:id="def-conditional-probability">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The probability of an event <m>F</m> given an event <m>E</m> has already occured is called the <alert> conditional probability </alert> of <m>F</m> given <m>E</m>. We write and compute it <md> \cal{P}[F|E] = \frac{\cal{P}[F \ \text{and} \ E]}{\cal{P}[E]}. </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="def-conditional-distribution">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>X,Y</m> be two discrete random variables and consider selecting a fixed outcome <m>x</m> from <m>X</m>. The <alert>conditional probability distribution</alert> of <m>Y</m> given <m>X</m> is the probability distribution generated by all possible <m>y</m> form <m>Y</m>. We write it <m>\cal{P}[x|Y]</m>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Do activity 13 problem 1a.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="def-contingency-table">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A <alert>contingency table</alert> shows all combinations of outcomes between two variables in an experiment. The <q>total row</q> and <q>total column</q> in this contingency table form the <alert>conditional distributions</alert> between the variables. When two variables have identical conditional distributions, we call them <alert> independent </alert>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Do activity 13 problem 1c.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-test-for-independence">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Ultimately, tests for independence are special cases of goodness-of-fit tests. We are claiming that a conditional probability distribution is the same both ways (two prob dists are equal). The independence between two random variables follows a <m>\chi^2</m> distribution with <m>(k_1-1)(k_2-1)</m> degrees of freedom where <m>k_1,k_2</m> are the number of outcomes from each random variable.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    
    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Do activity 13 problem 1d.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                An ecologist is testing the efficacy of a pollinator restoration technique. They have marked out 100 plots in a polluted meadow and treat half of the plots (selected at random). They then observe each plot for 5 minutes to determine whether a pollinator visits the plot. The data is summarised in the table below. 
            </p>
            <tabular>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Meadow Type</cell> <cell>Pollinator observed</cell> <cell>No Pollinator Observed</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Restored</cell> <cell>30</cell> <cell>20</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Unrestored</cell> <cell>20</cell> <cell>30</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
            <p>
                Test the efficacy of the treatment with 95% confidence.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
</section>
